        "title": "Dockerfile Instructions — Container Lifecycle and Networking",
        "content": "# Dockerfile Instructions — Container Lifecycle and Networking\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nThis lesson belongs to the 'Dockerfile Instructions' navigation topic in the DevMavox Docker learning path.\n\nA Dockerfile describes how an image is assembled. FROM selects the base image; RUN executes build commands; COPY and ADD place files into the image; WORKDIR selects the working directory; ARG and ENV configure build-time and runtime values; USER sets the execution identity; CMD and ENTRYPOINT define startup behavior. Instruction ordering affects image layers and cache reuse.\n\n## 2. Detailed Technical Explanation\n\nThe specific subject covered here is 'Dockerfile Instructions — Container Lifecycle and Networking'.\n\nExplain the Dockerfile instruction's syntax, purpose, build-time or runtime effect, input handling, generated image metadata, and interaction with other instructions. Discuss instruction ordering and practical mistakes that affect image correctness.\n\nAn engineer should identify the inputs consumed by this operation, the Docker component responsible for processing them, the state that is created or modified, and the expected output. Depending on the operation, the relevant components may include the Docker CLI, API, daemon, builder, image store, runtime, host kernel, registry, network, or storage driver.\n\n## 3. Practical Commands\n\nThe following commands provide a starting point for examining this area:\n\ndocker build --progress=plain -t devmavox-app:1.0 .\ndocker image inspect devmavox-app:1.0\ndocker history devmavox-app:1.0\n\nReplace example names and registry addresses with values from the target environment. Review command help and inspect the target before running commands that modify or delete resources. Commands shown here are diagnostic examples, not a substitute for environment-specific validation.\n\n## 4. Real DevOps Workflow\n\nIn a CI/CD pipeline, source code is checked out, build inputs are validated, a Docker image is produced, automated tests are executed, and the approved artifact is published to a registry. Deployment systems should consume the exact image that passed validation. Record the source commit, image tag, digest, build logs, test results, and release identifier to support traceability.\n\n## 5. Failure Analysis and Troubleshooting\n\nWhen this operation fails, capture the exact command, complete error message, timestamp, Docker client and server versions, and recent configuration changes. Determine whether the problem originates in configuration, permissions, daemon connectivity, build inputs, runtime behavior, host resources, networking, storage, or registry access. Test one root-cause hypothesis at a time, apply the smallest safe correction, and repeat the original validation.\n\n## 6. Production Best Practices\n\nUse maintained base images, controlled versions, trusted registries, least-privilege execution, protected credentials, and minimal runtime dependencies. Monitor container health, application logs, CPU, memory, filesystem usage, network behavior, registry availability, and build performance. Keep persistent data separate from disposable container state. Document recovery and rollback procedures before making production changes.\n\n## 7. Validation Checklist\n\n- Confirm the intended Docker context and engine.\n- Validate the relevant configuration and input files.\n- Execute the operation and capture its output.\n- Inspect the resulting Docker resource and metadata.\n- Test application behavior and required dependencies.\n- Review security, performance, and operational impact.\n- Record the result for future troubleshooting.\n\n## 8. Learning Outcome\n\nAfter studying 'Dockerfile Instructions — Container Lifecycle and Networking', an engineer should be able to explain its purpose within 'Dockerfile Instructions', describe the relevant Docker behavior, use appropriate commands, inspect the result, recognize common failure conditions, and apply suitable controls in a CI/CD and production environment.\n\n## Section Reference\n\nNavigation: Dockerfile Instructions\nSection: Dockerfile Instructions — Container Lifecycle and Networking\nSection ID: stage-8-topic-4-section-7"
      },
      {
