        "content": "# Docker Networking — BuildKit and Build Optimization\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nThis lesson belongs to the 'Docker Networking' navigation topic in the DevMavox Docker learning path.\n\nDocker networking connects containers to each other, the host, and external systems. Bridge, host, overlay, and other network drivers provide different connectivity models. Port publishing, DNS, routing, firewall rules, network membership, and isolation determine whether application components can communicate.\n\n## 2. Detailed Technical Explanation\n\nThe specific subject covered here is 'Docker Networking — BuildKit and Build Optimization'.\n\nExplain the builder selection, BuildKit execution model, frontend syntax, cache behavior, mount capabilities, and build output. Distinguish build-time secrets from image environment variables and explain why credentials must not be copied into image layers.\n\nAn engineer should identify the inputs consumed by this operation, the Docker component responsible for processing them, the state that is created or modified, and the expected output. Depending on the operation, the relevant components may include the Docker CLI, API, daemon, builder, image store, runtime, host kernel, registry, network, or storage driver.\n\n## 3. Practical Commands\n\nThe following commands provide a starting point for examining this area:\n\ndocker network ls\ndocker network create devmavox-net\ndocker network inspect devmavox-net\ndocker run -d --name web --network devmavox-net nginx\ndocker inspect web\n\nReplace example names and registry addresses with values from the target environment. Review command help and inspect the target before running commands that modify or delete resources. Commands shown here are diagnostic examples, not a substitute for environment-specific validation.\n\n## 4. Real DevOps Workflow\n\nIn a CI/CD pipeline, source code is checked out, build inputs are validated, a Docker image is produced, automated tests are execu