        "id": "stage-8-topic-11-section-6",
        "title": "Volumes and Storage — Multi-stage Image Construction",
        "content": "# Volumes and Storage — Multi-stage Image Construction\n\n## 1. Topic Context\n\nThis lesson belongs to the 'Volumes and Storage' navigation topic in the DevMavox Docker learning path.\n\nDocker storage includes the container writable layer, named volumes, bind mounts, and temporary filesystems. A container writable layer is tied to the container lifecycle, while named volumes can persist independently. Ownership, permissions, mount behavior, backup, and recovery are especially important for databases and stateful applications.\n\n## 2. Detailed Technical Explanation\n\nThe specific subject covered here is 'Volumes and Storage — Multi-stage Image Construction'.\n\nExplain how independent build stages are created, how artifacts move between stages, and why the final stage should contain only runtime requirements. Discuss target-stage builds, dependency separation, reproducibility, and image-size reduction.\n\nAn engineer should identify the inputs consumed by this operation, the Docker component responsible for processing them, the state that is created or modified, and the expected output. Depending on the operation, the relevant components may include the Docker CLI, API, daemon, builder, image store, runtime, host kernel, registry, network, or storage driver.\n\n## 3. Practical Commands\n\nThe following commands provide a starting point for examining this area:\n\ndocker volume ls\ndocker volume create devmavox-data\ndocker volume inspect devmavox-data\ndocker system df -v\n\nReplace example names and registry addresses with values from the target environment. Review command help and inspect the target before running commands that modify or delete resources. Commands shown here are diagnostic examples, not a substitute for environment-specific validation.\n\n## 4. Real DevOps Workflow\n\nIn a CI/CD pipeline, source code is checked out, build inputs are validated, a Docker image is produced, automated tests are executed, and the approved artifac